{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Men of holiness.</b> Because he mentioned your ox and your sheep, which are permitted for eating, he warned concerning meat that is torn, lest the poor person eat it because of his poverty." "And he mentioned 'in the field' only incidentally, and likewise the law regarding torn flesh applies also inside the city, and similar is the case of a nocturnal emission (Deuteronomy 23:11)." "<b>And to the dog.</b> The lamed is opened — for he [the dog] guards your ox and your sheep, and it is not fitting to eat it; rather only the dog may eat it. And R. Moses the Kohen said that torn flesh is more severe than carrion, for in torn flesh there is a kind of harmful poison that damages a person's offspring; therefore he [Moses] did not permit giving it to the ger who is in your gates, or selling it to a foreigner, as in the case of carrion."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {2 "In the last clause, 'he [Moses] did not permit,' the subject is understood as Moses (the lawgiver); alternatively the divine legislator is meant."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:37:33.189043935Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}